        "content": "<strong> The SM57 is among the most recorded microphones ever made. </strong>It handles guitar amps with authority that mics costing ten times as much can't match. The way it captures the crack and body of a snare drum, the growl of a cranked tube amp, the punch of a horn section. Drops in padded bags, falls off stages onto concrete, and kicks across festival stages still leave them working perfectly every time. The tight cardioid pattern gives excellent off-axis rejection, which means you can place one SM57 on a snare and another on the hi-hat without phase issues or bleed. On vocals, it delivers a raw, present, punchy sound that cuts through a dense mix â€” think Iggy Pop, think punk rock, think anything that needs attitude. Every studio needs at least two of these. Every musician should own one. There is no better value in all of audio.\n<p><strong>Pair it with:</strong> the SM57 ships bare, so grab <a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/dp/B001LNN2EQ?tag=topmusicg-20\" rel=\"nofollow sponsored\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"guide-link-btn\">a Mogami Gold Studio XLR cable</a> <a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0006PQOF4?tag=topmusicg-20\" rel=\"nofollow sponsored\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"guide-link-btn\">a un soporte K&M 210/2</a> <a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/dp/B079GXTKN3?tag=topmusicg-20\" rel=\"nofollow sponsored\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"guide-link-btn\">an Aston Shield GN pop filter</a> The moving-coil capsule shrugs off brutal SPL â€” a cranked stack, a snare rim, a horn section, so it's the safest mic you can point at anything loud.</p>",
